The rank of M_{n,m,p} is at most M_{n,m,p'} * M_{n',m,p} for appropriate decompositions, and there are submultiplicative properties.

Key insight: M_{n,m,p} ≤ M_{n,m,p'} ⊗ M_{n',m',p} type bounds. More precisely, if we can partition the indices:
- m = m1 + m2, then M_{n,m,p} ≤ M_{n,m1,p} + M_{n,m2,p}
- n = n1 + n2, then M_{n,m,p} ≤ M_{n1,m,p} + M_{n2,m,p}  
- p = p1 + p2, then M_{n,m,p} ≤ M_{n,m,p1} + M_{n,m,p2}

Also, M_{n,m,p} ≤ M_{n1,m1,p1} * M_{n2,m2,p2} if n=n1*n2, m=m1*m2, p=p1*p2 (tensor product).

